    "abstractText": "We propose The GANfather, an adversarial method without label requirements, to enhance the detection of illicit activity in various domains. It comprises a generator that produces meaningful attacks and a discriminator to detect them. The GANfather differs from anomaly detection as it generates attacks mimicking legitimate activity. It also differs from GANs as it focuses on generating out-ofsample data, namely examples of a novel class, such as illicit activity. We employ hyperparameter tuning to balance both goals. Optionally, we may encourage the generator to bypass pre-existing detection systems. This setup reveals defensive weaknesses for the discriminator to correct. We evaluate our method in two real-world use cases, money laundering, and recommendation systems. In the first, an estimated 0.7\u20133 trillion euros are laundered annually undetected. Our method moves cumulative amounts close to 250 thousand dollars through a network of accounts without being detected by existing systems. In the latter, we recommend the target item to a broad user base with as few as 30 synthetic attackers, corresponding to 0.5% of total users. In both cases, we train a new defence system to capture the synthetic attacks, potentially saving millions of dollars in detected criminal activity. Finally, our method is general and applies to other use cases targeted by adversarial attacks.",
